A||||10||JBFiero||Front Spark Plugs||||1||
Z||000000||JBFiero||10-23-2001||04:27 PM||||I just got a 86 GT with 10,000 miles it has been sitting since 1988. I have gotten it running and everything runs good. I went to change the plugs and the rear plugs came out with no problem. They were the orignal plugs.Went to change the front pluggs and cant get the socket to go down all the way on the plug. It is like the plug is to big or the hole is to small. The plug wires in the front are smooth and the rear wires are have ribs on them. Any ideas why i cant get them out? Thanks for your help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||WampusCat||10-23-2001||04:34 PM||||Not sure about the '86, but the 88's have an aluminum heat shield around the front plug wires.  The ribs on the wires fit the shape of the aluminum shields.  Maybe the heat shields stayed around the plug when you pulled the wires off?  Can you feel the plugs with your fingers?||||reg||1
Z||000002||JBFiero||10-23-2001||04:36 PM||||Yes I took the shileds off the wires and I can feel the plugs. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||10-23-2001||05:22 PM||||I had the same thing. There were leaves & dirt in the hole around the plugs. I took the shop vac out and tried to get in there as good as I could. I then had to push real hard on the socket to get it on.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000004||Patrick86GT||10-23-2001||05:58 PM||||I can reach em on mine w/ various extensions & universal.  <P>But, it's a hell of alot easier to change plugs and possible to clean up the front of the engine when I remove one side of the dog bone and I drop the 2 rear cradle bolts and let the engine cradle drop some supported on a floor jack, tilting it away from the fire wall. On pre-88, also have to take off one or two air blower tubes. Might sound involved, but it actually happens in minutes...particularly if you do it every year to keep the bolts mentioned freed up. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick86GT||10-23-2001||06:00 PM||||Also, congrats!  A 10,000 mile 86GT is a REAL find!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Two88GTs||10-23-2001||06:50 PM||||JBFiero, I just did the front plugs on my 64k 88GT which I bought last fall with 56k miles on it.  Prior to my ownership, it was last registered in 1992 with 42k on it.  More than likely, the same front plugs were in the car for 8 years with 14k of use.  Due to the heat sheield on the spark plug boots, there was sooooo much rust buildup on the spark plugs that a spark plug socket would not fit.  I had to scrape off the rust buildup with a small screwdriver before I could get the socket to fit.  As 88CoupeV6 said, you'll most likely find other debris as well.||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||10-23-2001||07:38 PM||||The other possibility especially if you said the plugs and wires were old is that the connector that "clicks" the wire to the plug is still on the plug. If the connector is still on the plug it would make it taller and you might not be able to get the socket all the way down. <BR>It won't be easy but the cap can usually be pried off or you can put the socket on the plug by itself. The rachet might have just enough space left to turn the socket.<P><BR>Hope it helps!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000008||87GTZ34||10-23-2001||09:50 PM||||JB,  don't be afraid to to take the decklid off.  If you weren't having issues then I would say there's no need to remove it to change the plugs.  It is better to lift it and see what's going on so you don't force something and then have to take the head off...  Just a suggestion though. GL||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||10-24-2001||07:48 AM||||could have a big rust buildup on the plugs if they been in there that long. soak them with WD40 overnite. May have to 'knock' the socket on slightly too grind the rust off.||||reg||1
Z||000010||roadwarrior||10-24-2001||10:32 AM||||just a guess in the dark since I can't see the car but maybe the gasket that is attached to the plug is sticking to the head, don't be afraid to tap it with a hammer, you might break off the insulator but the hexed part can only strip and that's why they invented vise grips.||||reg||1
